        "thinking": "Now I have a clearer picture of the architecture. The `cf-express-shim.ts` creates a router that captures routes in a specific format that `extractRoutesFromRouter` can read. But it only captures routes tagged with `__handler` (from `expressHandler`).\n\nFor `access.ts` routes that use raw Express handlers `(req, res) =\u003e { ... }`, these are NOT captured because they don't have `__handler` tags.\n\nThe key insight: The raw Express handlers in `access.ts` use Express-specific APIs:\n- `req.params`, `req.query`, `req.body` — Express request\n\n\n- `res.json()`, `res.status()`, `res.send()` — Express response\n- `req.actor` — custom property set by middleware\n\nRather than manually converting all these routes, I could create a proxy mechanism that mounts the raw Express handlers directly onto a CF-shimmed router, letting the shim handle the request/response translation automatically.\n\nBut looking at the CF Express shim, it's just a structural mock that captures the stack format without actually processing requests. The real flow in `app.ts` is: request comes in, builds resources, matches against compiled routes, resolves the actor context, then calls the handler with `RequestCtx`. The problem is that raw Express handlers expect Node-style `(req, res, next)` objects, not this context shape. Looking at the actual handlers in `access.ts`, they're reading from `req.params`, `req.query`, and `req.body` — so I'd need to bridge those property accesses somehow.\n\nInstead of trying to make the CF shim work, I should create a thin Express-to-CF adapter that builds fake `req` and `res` objects matching what the handlers expect, then passes them through to the raw Express handlers. I'd need to implement a `CfExpressRequest` with the properties the handlers use (`params`, `query`, `body`, `actor`, `method`, `path`, `headers`) and a `CfExpressResponse` that captures calls to `json()`, `status()`, and `send()` and converts them back to proper CF responses.\n\nThe tricky part is that `req.actor` gets resolved by CF's auth system, not Express middleware, so I'd need to handle that separately before invoking the handler. Also, the CF Express shim doesn't actually implement `router.handle()` - it just builds the routing structure - so I need to check what the actual bridge implementation does.\n\nLooking at the handlers in access.ts, they all follow a similar pattern with auth checks and database queries, so maybe instead of trying to fully bridge Express routing, I could create a wrapper that handles the common cases directly.\n\nBut with around 44 routes across these files—many with complex logic like permission checks and invite flows—manually implementing each one would be error-prone and risk missing edge cases. A better approach might be to modify access.ts itself to wrap handlers with expressHandler, which would let the existing route extraction pick them up automatically.\n\nLet me examine what expressHandler actually does...",
        "type": "thinking"
      }
    ],
